1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:10,083
Monday morning. You run git rebase main on
your branch, the way you do every Monday.

2
00:00:10,123 --> 00:00:14,502
It stops on the first commit, with
a conflict in ingest/loader.py.

3
00:00:14,842 --> 00:00:19,197
Your branch never touched ingest/loader.py.
You wrote a text cleaner.

4
00:00:19,237 --> 00:00:23,655
The loader is a colleague's work,
and it was merged to main on Friday.

5
00:00:23,995 --> 00:00:28,838
So why is Git asking you to resolve a
conflict in somebody else's file, in a commit

6
00:00:28,878 --> 00:00:33,779
you didn't write? Because of one word in
the definition of rebase, and one thing Git

7
00:00:33,819 --> 00:00:36,897
doesn't know about your
branch. Hold on to those two.

8
00:00:36,937 --> 00:00:39,426
You'll have both by the
end of the concept.

9
00:00:41,266 --> 00:00:45,093
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

10
00:00:45,133 --> 00:00:49,340
A rebase copies commits onto a new
base, and then moves the branch.

11
00:00:49,380 --> 00:00:53,777
So far, git rebase main has been one
command with one obvious meaning.

12
00:00:53,817 --> 00:00:58,784
This video takes it apart into its
arguments, because the obvious meaning is an

13
00:00:58,824 --> 00:01:00,686
accident of the simplest case.

14
00:01:01,026 --> 00:01:06,201
Every form of the command answers three
questions: which branch is rewritten, which

15
00:01:06,241 --> 00:01:10,159
commits are replayed, and on which
commit the copies are built.

16
00:01:10,199 --> 00:01:15,437
In git rebase main, the second and third
questions get the same answer, main, and so

17
00:01:15,477 --> 00:01:18,642
they look like one question.
--onto separates them.

18
00:01:18,982 --> 00:01:24,323
You'll see the forms of the command in one
table. Then three worked cases of --onto:

19
00:01:24,363 --> 00:01:29,641
the stacked branch from the hook, which is
a branch built on top of another branch,

20
00:01:29,681 --> 00:01:34,766
a fix moved sideways onto a release line,
and commits cut out of the middle of a

21
00:01:34,806 --> 00:01:39,955
branch. Then the two special forms: --root,
which rewrites from the first commit,

22
00:01:39,995 --> 00:01:43,735
and --keep-base, which rewrites
a branch without moving it.

23
00:01:45,575 --> 00:01:46,987
After this video you can:

24
00:01:47,327 --> 00:01:51,216
State which commits git rebase <upstream>
selects and where it puts them.

25
00:01:51,556 --> 00:01:56,744
Use git rebase --onto with three arguments
to cut a range and to move a branch

26
00:01:56,784 --> 00:01:57,347
sideways.

27
00:01:57,687 --> 00:02:02,373
Explain why a plain rebase of a stacked
branch replays commits that are not yours.

28
00:02:02,713 --> 00:02:08,088
Use --keep-base to rewrite a branch without
moving it, and --root to rewrite from

29
00:02:08,128 --> 00:02:09,224
the first commit.

30
00:02:11,064 --> 00:02:12,964
Three arguments, three roles.

31
00:02:13,304 --> 00:02:17,484
<branch> is the branch that gets
rewritten. Default: the current branch.

32
00:02:17,524 --> 00:02:20,884
If you name it, Git switches
to it as part of the command.

33
00:02:21,224 --> 00:02:25,590
<upstream> is only a boundary. The commits
replayed are <upstream>..<branch>:

34
00:02:25,630 --> 00:02:28,852
reachable from the branch, not
reachable from <upstream>.

35
00:02:28,892 --> 00:02:32,286
Reachable means you can get
there by following parent links.

36
00:02:32,626 --> 00:02:36,408
<newbase> is where the copies
are built. Default: <upstream>.

37
00:02:36,748 --> 00:02:41,059
Read the middle one again. The
upstream argument is only a boundary.

38
00:02:41,099 --> 00:02:46,434
It says where your commits begin, by exclusion:
everything it can reach isn't yours.

39
00:02:46,474 --> 00:02:50,977
It says nothing about where the copies
go, unless you leave --onto out.

40
00:02:51,317 --> 00:02:55,910
Here are the forms in one table. Read each
row as two answers: which commits are

41
00:02:55,950 --> 00:02:58,401
replayed, and what the
copies are built on.

42
00:02:58,741 --> 00:03:03,168
Now the reason for the hook. Rebase
replays everything not reachable from the

43
00:03:03,208 --> 00:03:06,243
upstream. "Reachable" is a
statement about the graph.

44
00:03:06,283 --> 00:03:10,305
Git has no record that your branch
"belongs on top of" another branch.

45
00:03:10,345 --> 00:03:14,946
A branch is a ref to a commit. "My commits"
is something only you can define, by

46
00:03:14,986 --> 00:03:19,588
naming a boundary. That's the one word,
reachable, and the one thing Git doesn't

47
00:03:19,628 --> 00:03:19,878
know.

48
00:03:20,218 --> 00:03:25,269
Most days the default boundary is right.
It goes wrong when the branch below yours

49
00:03:25,309 --> 00:03:30,050
lands on main in another form: squash-merged,
which means packed into one new

50
00:03:30,090 --> 00:03:35,203
commit, or rebased. Then main has the content
of those commits but not the commits.

51
00:03:35,243 --> 00:03:40,108
On screen that's 2b2840e, the squash. The
two loader commits below your two are

52
00:03:40,148 --> 00:03:44,579
still not reachable from main, so a
plain rebase puts them on your list.

53
00:03:44,919 --> 00:03:49,857
When not to use these forms? --root on a
repository that others have cloned is a

54
00:03:49,897 --> 00:03:54,772
rewrite of the whole project: the new
history and the old one have no commit in

55
00:03:54,812 --> 00:03:55,208
common.

56
00:03:57,048 --> 00:04:02,224
Read --onto as a sentence with three nouns:
"Take what lies after the old base, up

57
00:04:02,264 --> 00:04:05,023
to the tip, and rebuild
it on the new base."

58
00:04:05,363 --> 00:04:10,462
The textbook's analogy from two videos ago
still works: amendments redone against a

59
00:04:10,502 --> 00:04:15,602
new version of a contract. Extend it. A
plain rebase says: "everything of mine that

60
00:04:15,642 --> 00:04:20,309
the other party's current version does
not contain, redo it on their current

61
00:04:20,349 --> 00:04:25,325
version." --onto says: "my amendments are
these ones, starting after page U; redo

62
00:04:25,365 --> 00:04:29,722
them on document N." You name the
first page of your own work yourself.

63
00:04:30,062 --> 00:04:34,122
Where it breaks: in a contract, your
amendments are marked with your name.

64
00:04:34,162 --> 00:04:37,170
In Git the author field plays
no part in the selection.

65
00:04:37,210 --> 00:04:41,603
Commits are selected by reachability, and
commits written by a colleague will be

66
00:04:41,643 --> 00:04:44,928
replayed as readily as your own
if they're inside the range.

67
00:04:45,268 --> 00:04:50,610
So before any rebase with consequences,
run the command that prints the range: git

68
00:04:50,650 --> 00:04:55,729
log --oneline <upstream>..<branch>. If
a commit on that list isn't yours, your

69
00:04:55,769 --> 00:04:56,911
boundary is wrong.

70
00:04:58,751 --> 00:05:04,114
Before, on the left: B is the tip, and
x, y and z come after the old base, U.

71
00:05:04,154 --> 00:05:09,939
N is the new base. After, on the right:
three copies, marked with primes, on top of

72
00:05:09,979 --> 00:05:10,079
N.

73
00:05:10,419 --> 00:05:16,288
U itself isn't copied. x, y and z are. And
U stays where it was, with whatever else

74
00:05:16,328 --> 00:05:17,285
pointed at it.

75
00:05:19,125 --> 00:05:24,232
You started on main and you end up on
feat/rerank. Git doesn't check out the old tip

76
00:05:24,272 --> 00:05:29,258
of the branch on the way. It detaches at
main, makes the copies, and attaches HEAD

77
00:05:29,298 --> 00:05:33,364
to the branch at the end, which is
what the five reflog lines show.

78
00:05:33,704 --> 00:05:36,936
Without any argument, the branch
needs a configured upstream.

79
00:05:37,276 --> 00:05:41,090
Case 1: the stacked branch.
labs/run ch09/onto-stacked.

80
00:05:41,130 --> 00:05:44,733
feat/ingest-cleaner was
built on feat/ingest-loader.

81
00:05:44,773 --> 00:05:50,619
The loader work has since landed on main as
one squashed commit, with a small change

82
00:05:50,659 --> 00:05:51,950
made during review.

83
00:05:52,290 --> 00:05:57,344
Look at the graph. main has "Add
document loader (#41)" as 2b2840e.

84
00:05:57,384 --> 00:06:03,122
The branch below yours has the original
loader commits, 531d3da and 7fea52c.

85
00:06:03,162 --> 00:06:09,432
Same work, different commits. Predict: how
many commits will git rebase main try to

86
00:06:09,472 --> 00:06:12,701
replay? Say the number
out loud. I'll wait.

87
00:06:17,541 --> 00:06:22,341
Four. main..feat/ingest-cleaner lists your
two commits and the two loader commits

88
00:06:22,381 --> 00:06:27,181
below them. The second command, with the
loader branch as boundary, lists the two

89
00:06:27,221 --> 00:06:29,153
that belong to this branch alone.

90
00:06:29,493 --> 00:06:34,569
The plain rebase stops at once, at "1 of
4", with an add/add conflict in the loader

91
00:06:34,609 --> 00:06:38,946
file: it's trying to add a file that
the squashed commit already added.

92
00:06:38,986 --> 00:06:43,631
That's the hook, reproduced. The demo
aborts, and nothing had been resolved.

93
00:06:43,971 --> 00:06:47,122
Read the root-cause line:
Git knows reachability only.

94
00:06:47,462 --> 00:06:52,779
Say the fix as the three-noun sentence: new
base main, old base feat/ingest-loader,

95
00:06:52,819 --> 00:06:54,328
tip feat/ingest-cleaner.

96
00:06:54,668 --> 00:06:59,625
Two commits replayed, no conflict. Your two
commits sit on main, and the old loader

97
00:06:59,665 --> 00:07:02,214
commits are left aside
on their own branch.

98
00:07:02,554 --> 00:07:07,602
The same result as a graph: two copies on
top of main, and the loader branch exactly

99
00:07:07,642 --> 00:07:08,389
where it was.

100
00:07:08,729 --> 00:07:12,092
"Squash and merge" puts one
new commit on the base branch.

101
00:07:12,132 --> 00:07:15,611
The commits of the head branch
don't become ancestors of it.

102
00:07:15,651 --> 00:07:20,421
GitHub's documentation warns that continuing
to work on a head branch after it was

103
00:07:20,461 --> 00:07:25,231
squash-merged makes later pull requests
list the squashed commits again and repeat

104
00:07:25,271 --> 00:07:27,636
conflicts. Case 1 is the Git-side remedy.

105
00:07:27,976 --> 00:07:33,457
Case 2: sideways, onto an older
line. labs/run ch09/onto-sideways.

106
00:07:33,497 --> 00:07:38,979
fix/timeout was cut from main,
which already carries work for 2.0.

107
00:07:39,019 --> 00:07:42,074
The fix has to ship from release/1.4.

108
00:07:46,914 --> 00:07:52,178
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper.
Write the command before you see it.

109
00:07:52,218 --> 00:07:55,475
New base? Old base? Tip?
Then say it out loud.

110
00:08:00,315 --> 00:08:06,231
--onto release/1.4 main fix/timeout. main
served only as the boundary: "my commits

111
00:08:06,271 --> 00:08:08,047
are the ones after main".

112
00:08:08,387 --> 00:08:12,914
The two fix commits now hang off the
release, and the two 2.0 commits are on a

113
00:08:12,954 --> 00:08:13,734
separate line.

114
00:08:14,074 --> 00:08:19,384
The check confirms that one file differs
from the release and that the 2.0 client

115
00:08:19,424 --> 00:08:22,686
didn't come along: app
contains only retriever.py.

116
00:08:23,026 --> 00:08:27,764
Case 3: cutting commits out of the
middle. labs/run ch09/onto-cut.

117
00:08:27,804 --> 00:08:31,818
Two experiment commits sit in
the middle of feat/rerank.

118
00:08:36,958 --> 00:08:42,355
You want to keep the skeleton, drop the
two experiments, and keep the last two

119
00:08:42,395 --> 00:08:47,792
commits. Count from the tip: the tip is
~0, the experiments are ~2 and ~3, the

120
00:08:47,832 --> 00:08:51,695
skeleton is ~4. New base? Old
base? Predict the command.

121
00:08:56,535 --> 00:09:01,736
The copies are built on feat/rerank~4,
and the boundary feat/rerank~2 excludes

122
00:09:01,776 --> 00:09:05,297
everything up to and including
the second experiment.

123
00:09:05,637 --> 00:09:10,252
And as a graph: two copies on the skeleton,
and the two experiments left behind.

124
00:09:10,592 --> 00:09:15,116
The diff against ORIG_HEAD shows exactly
one file gone, the cross-encoder

125
00:09:15,156 --> 00:09:20,243
experiment. The same result comes from drop
in an interactive rebase, which is the

126
00:09:20,283 --> 00:09:23,244
next video. This form is
the one you can script.

127
00:09:23,584 --> 00:09:29,188
--root. labs/run ch09/rebase-root. Without
a boundary, the root commit itself is in

128
00:09:29,228 --> 00:09:32,724
the range, so even the first
commit can be reworded.

129
00:09:32,764 --> 00:09:38,368
This one is interactive. In the transcripts,
a small script plays the person at the

130
00:09:38,408 --> 00:09:42,924
keyboard, and prints the list as
Git opened it and as it was saved.

131
00:09:47,264 --> 00:09:53,783
pick became reword on the first line. Quick
quiz: how many of the three commits keep

132
00:09:53,823 --> 00:09:57,453
their ID? A, two. B, one.
C, none. Your answer?

133
00:10:02,293 --> 00:10:05,118
C, none. Every commit has a new ID.

134
00:10:05,458 --> 00:10:10,316
And git merge-base HEAD ORIG_HEAD exits
with status 1: the new history and the old

135
00:10:10,356 --> 00:10:12,049
one have no commit in common.

136
00:10:12,389 --> 00:10:19,414
--keep-base. labs/run ch09/keep-base.
Tidy a branch without catching up.

137
00:10:19,754 --> 00:10:23,787
main has moved on by one
commit. The branch has a fixup!

138
00:10:23,827 --> 00:10:29,314
commit to fold in, a correction that
names the earlier commit it belongs to.

139
00:10:29,354 --> 00:10:31,205
The merge base is 8afc6bd.

140
00:10:31,545 --> 00:10:36,493
The progress counter starts at 2. Instruction
1, a pick whose parent is already the

141
00:10:36,533 --> 00:10:41,481
base, needed no new commit: Git fast-forwards
over instructions that would recreate

142
00:10:41,521 --> 00:10:42,203
what exists.

143
00:10:42,543 --> 00:10:47,514
The fixup! commit was folded into its
target, and three commits remain.

144
00:10:47,854 --> 00:10:52,114
The branch still forks from 8afc6bd
although main has moved on.

145
00:10:52,154 --> 00:10:54,366
Same merge base before and after.

146
00:10:56,206 --> 00:10:57,855
Five mistakes to watch for.

147
00:10:58,195 --> 00:11:03,159
Reading <upstream> as "the place my commits
go". Root cause: it is only a boundary;

148
00:11:03,199 --> 00:11:07,260
the destination is <newbase>, which
merely defaults to the upstream.

149
00:11:07,600 --> 00:11:12,008
Running a plain git rebase main on a
stacked branch after the branch below was

150
00:11:12,048 --> 00:11:16,513
squash-merged. Root cause: the lower
commits are not reachable from main, which

151
00:11:16,553 --> 00:11:20,391
holds their content in a different
commit, so they are in the range.

152
00:11:20,731 --> 00:11:25,938
Getting the order of the --onto arguments
wrong. Root cause: the first argument is

153
00:11:25,978 --> 00:11:31,186
where copies are built, the second is the
excluded boundary; swapping them replays

154
00:11:31,226 --> 00:11:33,490
the wrong range onto the wrong base.

155
00:11:33,830 --> 00:11:38,746
Tidying and catching up in one push. Root
cause: reviewers cannot separate what

156
00:11:38,786 --> 00:11:43,890
changed in your commits from a week of
upstream changes; --keep-base keeps the two

157
00:11:43,930 --> 00:11:44,267
apart.

158
00:11:44,607 --> 00:11:48,978
Using --root on a shared repository
to fix the first commit message.

159
00:11:49,018 --> 00:11:54,037
Root cause: every commit gets a new ID,
and the old and new histories share no

160
00:11:54,077 --> 00:11:54,491
commit.

161
00:11:56,331 --> 00:12:01,117
Now, out of the lab. A data-pipeline
team works in stacks: a loader branch, a

162
00:12:01,157 --> 00:12:04,502
cleaner branch on top of it,
a chunker on top of that.

163
00:12:04,542 --> 00:12:09,705
Their host squash-merges pull requests.
Every time the bottom of a stack lands, the

164
00:12:09,745 --> 00:12:12,338
branch above it is in the state of Case 1.

165
00:12:12,678 --> 00:12:17,288
Before the team understood this, the
Monday rebase was a ritual of resolving

166
00:12:17,328 --> 00:12:21,878
conflicts in files nobody on the upper
branch had touched, and occasionally

167
00:12:21,918 --> 00:12:26,651
resolving them wrongly. Now the rule is
in the pull request template: when the

168
00:12:26,691 --> 00:12:31,669
branch below yours lands in another form,
rebase with --onto, naming the old base.

169
00:12:32,009 --> 00:12:36,997
And for review, the textbook's advice:
reviewers of a force-pushed branch want to

170
00:12:37,037 --> 00:12:41,901
know what changed in your commits. If one
push both tidies and catches up, that

171
00:12:41,941 --> 00:12:46,867
answer is mixed with a week of upstream
changes. Tidy with --keep-base and push.

172
00:12:46,907 --> 00:12:51,833
Catch up with a plain rebase and push
again. Each push then has one explanation.

173
00:12:53,673 --> 00:12:59,662
Your turn. Do Lab 9.2, "Transplant
a branch with --onto", in

174
00:12:59,702 --> 00:13:02,174
lab-manual/m09-rebase.md.

175
00:13:02,514 --> 00:13:03,951
Before you type the rebase:

176
00:13:04,291 --> 00:13:09,379
Draw the graph from git log
--oneline --graph --all.

177
00:13:09,719 --> 00:13:13,019
Mark the new base, the old base
and the tip on your drawing.

178
00:13:13,359 --> 00:13:18,479
Write the output you expect from git log
--oneline <old base>..<tip>: this is the

179
00:13:18,519 --> 00:13:23,702
list of commits that will be copied. Run
it and compare before you run the rebase.

180
00:13:24,042 --> 00:13:30,394
The challenge is Exercise 9.9, Level 4,
"the morning rebase that replays somebody

181
00:13:30,434 --> 00:13:34,577
else's commits", in
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

182
00:13:36,417 --> 00:13:41,636
Q154: "git rebase main on a feature branch
conflicts in a file the branch never

183
00:13:41,676 --> 00:13:46,497
touched. Give two different root causes
and the command that fixes each."

184
00:13:48,837 --> 00:13:53,303
Answer out loud first. A strong answer
begins with the one command that turns the

185
00:13:53,343 --> 00:13:57,365
symptom into evidence: the list of
commits the rebase is about to replay.

186
00:13:57,405 --> 00:14:01,983
From there it gives two causes that are
different in mechanism, not two wordings of

187
00:14:02,023 --> 00:14:06,489
one, and for each names the fixing command
with its arguments in the right roles.

188
00:14:06,829 --> 00:14:11,387
Today's video gave you one of the two in
full. The other follows from the same

189
00:14:11,427 --> 00:14:16,337
definition of the range. Think about what
else can put a commit you didn't write, or

190
00:14:16,377 --> 00:14:19,284
a change that's already
upstream, into main..HEAD.

191
00:14:21,124 --> 00:14:24,051
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

192
00:14:24,391 --> 00:14:28,829
Every rebase answers three questions: which
branch is rewritten, which commits are

193
00:14:28,869 --> 00:14:31,068
replayed, and where the copies are built.

194
00:14:31,408 --> 00:14:36,147
The upstream argument is only a boundary:
the replayed commits are those reachable

195
00:14:36,187 --> 00:14:38,012
from the branch and not from it.

196
00:14:38,352 --> 00:14:42,984
--onto <new base> <old base> <tip> separates
the boundary from the destination,

197
00:14:43,024 --> 00:14:47,775
which is what a stacked branch needs after
the branch below it was squash-merged,

198
00:14:47,815 --> 00:14:51,087
and it also moves a branch
sideways or cuts a range out.

199
00:14:51,427 --> 00:14:56,937
--keep-base rebuilds on the existing merge
base, so a branch can be tidied without

200
00:14:56,977 --> 00:14:57,749
catching up.

201
00:14:58,089 --> 00:15:02,466
--root includes the first commit and
leaves no commit in common with the old

202
00:15:02,506 --> 00:15:02,931
history.

203
00:15:04,771 --> 00:15:07,376
Read section 9.5 of Chapter 9.

204
00:15:07,716 --> 00:15:13,161
Do Exercise 9.5, Level 2, "a
commit that main already has", in

205
00:15:13,201 --> 00:15:16,080
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

206
00:15:16,420 --> 00:15:21,260
That Monday conflict in somebody else's
file is no longer a puzzle: you can print

207
00:15:21,300 --> 00:15:24,754
the range, see the wrong
